\f5\fs26 The foyer of the Pre-emptive Reciprocity and Pro-active Euthanasia Division of Termocorp was quiet and dignified as usual. An ever-changing hologram of the CEO stood vigil over company crest etched into the floor of the entrance hall, while dark suits and sensibly cut skirts scurried about busily. The only outward acknowledgment of each other\'92s existence was the nifty footwork employed to evade collisions, thereby avoiding arguments and violent recriminations. \
Damien Hardman had moved through middle management briskly and his present job description elevated him to a prestigious position, but his current office displeased him immensely. Promotion meant an office on the upper floors and he had hoped to leapfrog the layer, but instead it had landed him smack bang in the centre of it. He had no view, the white haze of the layer rendering his window nothing more than a necrotic mirror that slowly turned green as the fetid, septic air tarnished the thick glass. Even though he knew turning out the lights and squashing his nose against the pane made little difference, adding only a few centimeters of visibility, he tried it again anyway. No matter how many times he did it, the view did not change. He tramped back to his desk and illuminated the room. \
Avi had not reported in, but Damien wasn\'92t too concerned. He knew her little quirk of getting laid after finishing a job and he figured she was still hosing herself down after an all-nighter. He thought it was a little ghoulish to fuck so soon after killing someone, but opined that it was something to do with the interconnectedness of life and death \'96 the circle of life bullshit. He wondered if death made women want to fuck, it would certainly explain why he never went home alone from a funeral, but he rejected the notion that killing aroused Avi. Her psych profile stated precisely the opposite was true, killing neither excited her nor caused her anxiety; she was emotion-neutral. She was probably just molested as a child, he concluded. \
The device she planted had gone off without a hitch but, he could not work out why the water had been diverted. It seemed very likely the events were connected \'96 an explosion and fire immediately followed by an attack on the water supply. How had Avi managed both? The CEO had apparently consumed half a litre of recyc and was screaming blue murder; luckily, some bonehead had drowned himself in public baths and been erroneously labelled the terrorist. This was quite a stroke of luck in an otherwise sticky state of affairs. Scapegoat was his favourite meat and this fat bloater had been laid squarely at his feet. He would still have a few curly questions to answer, so he needed to act quickly. \
He touched an icon and the plain face of his secretary popped up on his holo-screen. \'93Have you managed to rouse Avi yet?\'94 \
\'93No answer, sir. I\'92ve tried her communicator, hard line, link line and I\'92ve rattled her implant so many times her frontal lobes should be jelly. She doesn\'92t answer.\'94\
\'93Where is she?\'94\
\'93Satellite tracking says she\'92s at home.\'94\
\'93Have you sent anyone around?\'94\
\'93No. I had not thought of that.\'94\
\'93Do it now.\'94 He ordered abruptly. Technology really made people lazy and stupid. No-one, it seemed, could be bother to get off there ever-widening backsides and physically do something, relying almost exclusively on surveillance devices and electronic trickery. He sighed and spun around on his chair to check out the view. He sighed again. 
\f6\i How could I forget? No fucking view. 
\f5\i0 He touched the screen again. \'93Harmony, who have you got going?\'94 \
\'93No-one yet.\'94\
\'93Don\'92t worry, I\'92ll go.\'94 He said, scantly disguising exasperation.\
Half an hour later he entered Avi\'92s flat. All seemed fine; carpets recently vacuumed, cushions neatly placed on the sofa, kitchen sparkling and an annoyingly pleasant view out of all windows. The bedroom was similarly spotless; bed made up with fresh crisp sheets, hospital corners perfectly folded. As he entered the bathroom, however, he caught the minute scent of something very familiar \'96 blood commencing to rot. His pistol was out in an instant, index finger resting lightly on the trigger guard, his eyes scanning the room in quadrants. Avi and some unknown woman were in the bath, no pulse and cold, congealing blood pooled around them. 
\f6\i Looks like you brought the wrong one home this time, Avi. Who the fuck is this other one?
\f5\i0 \
He called it in and within minutes, a sanitising team arrived to remove all evidence of Avi ever having been there. The concealed armoury cabinet and the tiny makeshift workshop was carefully cleared of booby traps, weapons, explosives and all manner of electronic gadgets, some of which would warrant later study. It looked like Avi had manufactured a few ingenious improvised devices. The bodies were carved into pieces, removed in several duffel bags before the entire premises was steamed, disinfected and fumigated. They were deposited in a furnace along with all of Avi\'92s belongings, everything. The entire operation taking less than 1 hour. The company assassin name Avi and her unknown friend never existed.\
Damien had barely planted his behind in his office chair when summoned to the top floor. Very rarely is it a good thing to get an invitation to see the Director and he seemed none too pleased when Damien entered the cavernous office. \
\'93I want a report on the nightclub debacle.\'94 The Director blurted out.\
\'93Well, sir, as directed by your office we arranged for a device to be planted in the Stick it Inn, a nightclub in the inner west. Agent Avi was dispatched and planted the device. Somehow the water infrastructure was compromised and water meant for the lows was redirected to the north side.\'94\
\'93How was the water compromised?\'94\
\'93That is unknown, but according to reports, Alexander Shakti sorted out the problem and got the water running in the right direction.\'94 He said, waiting for the shock to hit the Director. The legend of Shakti was known far and wide; he created problems, he did not solve them.\
\'93Shakti? That idiot doesn\'92t know how to wipe his own arse.\'94\
\'93It seems unlikely he could have fixed it, some engineers are taking the credit. We haven\'92t been able to identify all of them yet.\'94\
\'93Identify them and report back to me. Do not contact them. Do not terminate them. This is a Chairman\'92s Directive.\'94\
\'93Yes, sir.\'94\
\'93I want Avi\'92s contract terminated.\'94 The director said gravely.\
\'93I have already taken care of that. She\'92s been wrapped and roasted.\'94 He said, hoping this would please the boss. He thought better of complicating the issue by telling him about the other girl they found. If she ever mattered, she didn\'92t anymore.\
\'93Good. That should satisfy the CEO. Now get out of my sight.\'94 The director was impressed. Before the young upstart entered, he had half-considered terminating his contract. Clearly, they had promoted the right man \'96 the operation had gone awry, but he had acted decisively. He immediately entered a few key strokes in his comms device and a hologram of the CEO appeared, illuminating the desk with a gentle glow. \
\'93What happened?\'94 The CEO demanded. \'93It was a simple job. How could it go so badly?\'94\
\'93I have just spoken to the agent in charge, Damien Hardman. The worker he dispatched was one of our best operatives, Avi and she had never failed us before. She seems to have placed the device correctly, the explosion was on time and devastated the entire building. They are going to have to demolish and rebuild. We are still in the early stages, but we have not identified the cleaner among the dead. He may have been blown to pieces or he may have escaped.\'94 The Director was in full damage control mode. \'93As directed, sir, neither Damien nor Avi knew the identity of the target. That still remains between you and I.\'94 \
\'93Keep it that way Mr Director. How did the water get switched off? You know level sevens were drinking fucking recyc don\'92t you?\'94 The CEO never used coarse language unless he was livid. \'93Could this Avi woman be involved?\'94\
\'93I don\'92t think so, she was meticulous and a true professional. She loved killing, I mean she really loved it. It\'92s unlikely she would do anything to upset the apple-cart. She took pride in her work and did everything by the numbers. Nevertheless, I had her contract terminated. If she was a liability, she is no longer.\'94 He hoped that sounded sufficiently managerial for the CEO. \
\'93Good. At least some action has been taken. She won\'92t be a problem now.\'94\
\'93Agent Hardman is looking into the source of the vandalism at the public baths near Erskintown. It seems that it was tampered with on-site, but with some really tricky algorithms, something the IT boys had not encountered before. There are vast reams of data missing, including all security footage for several blocks in every direction. Whoever these terrorists are, they are very good.\'94\
\'93You will have to be better, Mr. Director.\'94 The CEO always looked for opportunities to slap the director down. \'93I hope Agent Harman is up to the challenge. I expect results and I expect them fast.\'94\
\'93What about the target, Sir. Should we go looking for him?\'94\
\'93No. If he is alive, just let him be. Do not approach him and do not arrest.\'94 The CEO was most adamant. \
\'93Apart from me, no one knows who he is.\'94 The director said with relief. He knew he would not blab, so nobody under his command will have any reason to speak to the poor sap. For whatever reason, a cleaner had come under the adverse attention of the CEO and that meant his life expectancy was now quite short.\
\'93Yes. You are the only one.\'94 At that very moment the CEO decided the Director had to go. He was an intolerable security risk. He was kicking himself, though, he should have used a blank for this job, but he had erroneously figured his army of assassins would be able to handle a simple bombing. Yes, the director definitely had to go. He picked up a small device and depressed the button. Immediately he saw the Director\'92s Office Manager enter the room with a Particle Pulse Pistol, she leveled it at the director\'92s head and fired without hesitation. The Director slumped dead on his desk and she then put the gun to her temple and fired.\
\'93Excellent.\'94 The hologram said and then disappeared. He did not wish to burn such a reliable asset as Janine Wildsmith, she had provided high quality intelligence for years, but she knew what she was getting into when she agreed to be a sleeper agent and was well aware that she would have to make this sacrifice when called upon. She was loyal to her Director and it had taken him years to turn her, but the birth of her intellectually impaired son found her swimming in debt. To protect the health system from undue costs, when identified, the retarded and deformed were destroyed in utero, but somehow the tests had not picked anything up. She came to him begging for financial relief, precisely as he had predicted when he had her medical records altered. 
